    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d47">

                <pb facs="0160" n="54" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg54"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Kimberly to Mr.
                            Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States in Central America</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1891-01-26"><hi rendition="#italic">January
                                26, 1891</hi>.</date> (Received February 13.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 239.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Learning a new minister for our Government
                    is to presently appear here, I have the honor to suggest that while peace and
                    tranquillity are at present reigning that he be advised to arrange, if possible,
                    a better and safer system of telegraphic communication with our home Government
                    at Washington. These telegraphic lines being under the ownership and
                    surveillance of this Government should make them all the more amenable under our
                    treaty laws for the safe conduct, prompt and easy access to and from your agents
                    here.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Samuel Kimberly</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Chargé d’Affaires ad int</hi>.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
